---
import ContentLayout from '../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';

const asideLinks = [
  { label: 'Summary', href: '#summary' },
  { label: 'Information you send', href: '#information-you-send' },
  { label: 'Guide feedback', href: '#guide-feedback' },
  { label: 'Server logs and security', href: '#server-logs-and-security' },
  { label: 'Cookies and analytics', href: '#cookies-and-analytics' },
  { label: 'Service providers', href: '#service-providers' },
  { label: 'Retention', href: '#retention' },
  { label: 'Your rights', href: '#your-rights' },
  { label: 'Contact', href: '#contact' },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Privacy policy"
  metaTitle="Privacy Policy: What Data This Site Handles"
  description="What FixProblemNow collects through contact messages and anonymous guide feedback, how it is used, which services may process it, and your privacy rights."
  canonical="/privacy-policy/"
  eyebrow="Privacy"
  intro="This policy describes the limited information the site handles: a message you choose to send, anonymous guide feedback, and standard server data needed to run and protect the service."
  updated="2026-11-15"
  asideLinks={asideLinks}
  asideNote="This page describes the site as built. It is not a claim of certification or a substitute for legal advice."
>
  <h2 id="summary">Summary</h2>
  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>No account is required to read any guide.</li>
    <li>No advertising, analytics, tracking pixels, or social embeds are used.</li>
    <li>Contact details are requested only when you choose to send a message.</li>
    <li>Guide feedback records the guide, category, yes/no answer, and time — not your identity.</li>
    <li>You can ask about, correct, or request deletion of a message you sent.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="information-you-send">Information you send</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/contact/">contact form</a> asks for your name, email address, topic, optional guide
    reference, and message. The name and email are used to understand and reply to the message. The
    topic helps route a correction, accessibility report, or privacy request. The guide reference
    helps locate the page you are writing about.
  </p>
  <p>
    Do not include passwords, one-time verification codes, recovery keys, full payment-card numbers,
    or other sensitive information in a message. The site does not need them to investigate a guide
    problem. If you accidentally send such information, use the contact form to ask for its deletion.
  </p>
  <p>
    Contact messages are delivered through a configured email provider or webhook. The provider
    processes the message on behalf of the site operator. The site does not sell contact details or
    use them for marketing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="guide-feedback">Guide feedback</h2>
  <p>
    Each guide includes a “Was this guide helpful?” control with Yes and No options. When you use it,
    the server records the guide identifier, category, whether the answer was helpful, and the time.
    It does not ask for your name, email address, or a free-text comment. A random identifier is not
    created for you.
  </p>
  <p>
    The browser stores a timestamp in local storage under a key such as
    <code>fpn-feedback:guide-name</code> so the same browser does not submit the same feedback twice.
    That value stays on your device and is not sent with the feedback. Clearing site data removes it.
    Local storage is not a cookie and is not used for advertising or cross-site tracking.
  </p>

  <h2 id="server-logs-and-security">Server logs and security</h2>
  <p>
    The hosting provider and web server may record standard technical data such as the requested
    URL, time, browser user-agent, and IP address. This data is used to deliver pages, diagnose
    errors, and protect the service from abuse. The application does not write contact message
    bodies or email addresses to its error log.
  </p>
  <p>
    To limit form abuse, the server keeps a short-lived in-memory counter keyed by IP address. The
    counter contains a request count and reset time, is held only for the rate-limit window (about
    ten minutes), and is not used to build a profile. A distributed deployment may also apply rate
    limiting at the edge.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cookies-and-analytics">Cookies and analytics</h2>
  <p>
    The site does not set advertising or analytics cookies. It does not use Google Analytics,
    advertising pixels, session replay, heatmaps, or social media embeds that track you across sites.
    Fonts are served from this domain rather than a third-party font service.
  </p>
  <p>
    The hosting provider may use strictly necessary cookies or similar technology for security and
    load balancing. Those are controlled by the host, not by the site’s content code.
  </p>

  <h2 id="service-providers">Service providers</h2>
  <p>
    Depending on how this deployment is configured, the following categories of provider may process
    data:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hosting and infrastructure.</strong> Serves pages and may keep standard server logs.</li>
    <li><strong>Email delivery.</strong> If configured, an email API such as Resend delivers contact messages.</li>
    <li><strong>Webhook endpoint.</strong> If configured, contact or feedback records are forwarded to an endpoint chosen by the site operator.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These providers may process data in countries other than yours. Where required, the site
    operator is responsible for putting appropriate transfer safeguards in place. The exact
    providers active on a deployment are determined by its environment configuration, which is
    documented for the operator in the project README.
  </p>

  <h2 id="retention">Retention</h2>
  <p>
    Contact messages are kept only as long as needed to handle the message, investigate a
    correction, or meet a legal obligation. Feedback records are kept in aggregate or record form
    for as long as they are useful for prioritising guide updates. Rate-limit counters expire
    automatically. Server logs are retained according to the hosting provider’s configuration.
  </p>

  <h2 id="your-rights">Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Where data protection law applies to you, you may have the right to ask for a copy of the
    personal data held about you, to correct it, to delete it, to restrict or object to certain
    processing, and to complain to a supervisory authority. The site does not make automated
    decisions with legal or similarly significant effects, and it does not profile visitors for
    advertising.
  </p>
  <p>
    To make a request, use the <a href="/contact/?topic=privacy">contact form</a> and choose the
    privacy topic. Include enough information to identify the message you sent; do not send a
    password or identity document unless it is specifically requested through a verified channel.
    A request may need to be verified before it can be completed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="children">Children</h2>
  <p>
    The site is a general technology reference and is not directed at children under 13. Contact
    details should be submitted by a parent, guardian, or someone old enough to manage their own
    personal data. If a message from a child is identified, it will be deleted on request.
  </p>

  <h2 id="security">Security</h2>
  <p>
    The site uses HTTPS in production, validates form input, limits request rates, sets security
    headers, and avoids logging sensitive message content. No internet service can promise absolute
    security. If you believe you have found a security issue, use the contact form and choose the
    privacy topic, and describe the issue without exploiting it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="changes">Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    If the way the site handles data changes, this page will be updated and the date at the top will
    change. Material changes will be described in plain language rather than hidden in a revision
    history.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Privacy questions and requests can be sent through the
    <a href="/contact/?topic=privacy">contact form</a>. See also the
    <a href="/terms/">terms and conditions</a> and <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>
